WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.251 --> 00:00:04.200
(wistful piano music)

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.670
- When I walk into Skid Row,

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.050
I'm overwhelmed with guilt and shame

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:15.000
that a place like Skid Row
exists in walking distance

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.139
of one of the greatest
concert halls in the world.

00:00:18.139 --> 00:00:20.970
(uplifting music)

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:24.070
For the people who experience
chronic homelessness,

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.730
music is not a form of entertainment.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:27.853
It's a lifeline.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.620
And when we make music in Skid Row,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.550
it's not about doing
something for the homeless.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.733
It's about understanding our neighbor.

00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:43.894
(lively symphonic music)

00:00:43.894 --> 00:00:45.670
Making music at Walt Disney Concert Hall

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.690
with the LA Philharmonic is sacred to me.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.950
I get to sit with some
of the greatest musicians

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:56.070
in the country and present
something transcendent

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.960
to an audience, almost like

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.276
I'm putting something on a pedestal.

00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:03.866
(exciting symphonic music)

00:01:03.866 --> 00:01:06.220
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.680
And making music in Skid Row

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.493
is actually about taking that
product off the pedestal.

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:16.072
(soothing chamber music)

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.220
Street Symphony is an initiative

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.700
that brings live musical performances,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.610
teaching artistry, and storytelling

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.330
to people who are impacted

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.283
by homelessness and
incarceration in LA County.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.270
It's comprised of some
of the greatest musicians

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.330
living and working in Los Angeles today;

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:44.570
and, to date, has performed
over 500 free concerts

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.690
in Skid Row clinics and shelters

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:50.040
as well as in all five
Los Angeles County jails.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.140
It's as much our job to
play in the concert hall

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.617
as it is to play in Skid Row.

00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:56.441
(lively chamber music)

00:01:56.441 --> 00:02:00.358
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:02:06.844 --> 00:02:10.177
(plaintive violin solo)

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.360
I can't talk about the
origins of Street Symphony

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.470
without talking about Nathaniel Ayers,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.563
who's a subject of a book and
a movie called "The Soloist."

00:02:20.563 --> 00:02:23.896
(plaintive violin solo)

00:02:25.203 --> 00:02:26.770
Nathaniel was one of the first Black men

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.560
to ever study at The Julliard School.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.810
And in his junior year, he was diagnosed

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.670
with paranoid schizophrenia,
and he ended up living

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.990
in the Second Street
tunnel in walking distance

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.390
of Walt Disney Concert Hall.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.640
When I joined the LA Philharmonic,

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.940
I was asked to become
Nathaniel's violin teacher.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.930
And then eventually we started

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.893
having these lessons in Skid Row.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.540
I started asking the questions

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.200
of how many more Nathaniels are out here?,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.990
because every single
person deserves access

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.263
to their creative and expressive life.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.353
It's our job to show up to
find who those voices are.

00:03:07.438 --> 00:03:10.771
(plaintive violin solo)

00:03:14.265 --> 00:03:17.182
(slow piano music)

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.770
For people who have experienced pain,

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.230
the arts are a redemptive connection

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.840
back to feeling like their
stories and their lives

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.403
and their neighborhoods matter.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.490
Every Street Symphony event
has a performative aspect

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.260
but also a chance for the audiences

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.460
to tell their stories,
to tell us performers

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.526
how they've received that music.

00:03:50.526 --> 00:03:53.110
(slow symphonic music)

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.300
In that moment, I'm just
a man standing there

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.140
with a violin, and all I have to offer

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.215
through that instrument
is my fullest humanity.

00:04:03.215 --> 00:04:06.465
(slow symphonic music)

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:12.649
(octave bell tones)

